  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nIssued at 216 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nSurface winds behind the frontal passage will become northerly\non Monday, with northwesterly flow aloft ushering in cooler\ntemperatures and a brief relief from the heat. Afternoon highs\nwill range in the 80s for a majority of the forecast area, with\nsome locations closer to the MO/AR border and over far southeast\nKS reaching the low 90s. There exists some isolated chances\n(20-40%) for convection Monday afternoon over the far southern\nportion of MO and into AR as some upper level energy pushes\nthrough, which could limit the temperatures even further. Any \nactivity that does enter the CWA should remain sub-severe.\n\nSoutherly flow returns on Tuesday, bringing warming temperatures\nback to the area. Highs will range in the low to mid 90s, \nreaching the upper 90s to low 100s towards far southeast \nKS/southwest MO, with heat index values in the mid 90s to low \n100s. Wednesday will likely be a few degrees warmer, however \nlarge spreads between the NBMs 25th and 75th percentiles leads \nto lowered confidence in exact temperatures. \n\nAn upper level shortwave trough is progged to make its way through\nthe region Wednesday night into Thursday, with an associated \nsurface cold front passing through. This will bring additional \nchances (30-50%) for showers/storms. There's still much\nuncertainty as we look ahead towards the end of the week, as any\nsmall change will affect temperatures and precipitation chances.\nThe current forecast temperatures show highs in the mid 80s\nnortheast to the mid 90s southwest to finish out the week,\nhowever this could change as models get a better handle on\nfuture systems.",
